. Their life history was a mystery for thousands of years, and mating in the wild has not yet been observed. The five stages of their development were originally thought to be different species. They are critically endangered due to hydroelectric dams, overfishing by fisheries on coasts for human consumption, and parasites."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-european-eel"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_eel"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["German cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisine",[["Eel soup","dish/eel-soup"]]],["Thuringian cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisine-thuringia",[["Eel soup","dish/eel-soup"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Eel as food","ingredient/eel-as-food"]]}]},"dish/egg-wash":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/egg-wash","n":"Egg wash","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["An egg wash is beaten eggs, sometimes mixed with another liquid such as water or milk, which is brushed onto the surface of a pastry before baking or breading. Egg washes can also be used on calzones or on fish."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-egg-wash"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Egg_wash"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine"],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Cream","ingredient/cream"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Pardon Your French","ref":"site/pardonyourfrench-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"pardonyourfrench.com","note":"Audrey Le Goff, a French cook, shares classic and regional French home recipes.","url":"https://pardonyourfrench.com","suggested":true},{"name":"La Cuisine de Géraldine","ref":"site/lacuisinedegeraldine-fr","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"lacuisinedegeraldine.fr","no