nt/doubanjiang","n":"Doubanjiang","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Doubanjiang, also known as douban, toban-djan, broad bean chili sauce, or fermented chili bean paste, is a hot and savory Chinese bean paste made from fermented broad beans (fava beans), chili peppers, soybeans, salt, and flour. Characteristically used in Sichuan cuisine, it has been called \"the soul of Sichuan cuisine.\" Sichuan dishes such as mapo tofu, huoguo (Sichuan hotpot), yuxiang flavoring, and shuizhu all use doubanjiang as a key ingredient. Other regions have their own versions: in Guangdong and Taiwan, for instance, Sichuan doubanjiang is called la-doubanjiang (Chinese: 辣豆瓣醬, \"la\" (辣) meaning \"hot\" or \"spicy\") to distinguish it from plainer  and milder versions."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-doubanjiang"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doubanjiang"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine",[["Mapo tofu","dish/mapo-tofu"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Douchi","ingredient/douchi"],["Tofu","ingredient/tofu"]]}]},"ingredient/canning":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/canning","n":"Canning","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 4 cuisines","intro":[["Canning is a method of food preservation in which food is processed and sealed in an airtight container (jars like Mason jars, and steel and tin cans). Canning provides a shelf life that typically ranges from one to five years, although under specific circumstances, it can be much longer. A freeze-dried canned product, such as canned dried lentils, could last as long as 30 years in an edible state."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-canning"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canning"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","coun